      <Xref identifier="P42043" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="600" centerY="395" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">The cDNA encoding ferrochelatase I (FC1), one out of two ferrochelatases found in the genome of &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt; has been isolated and partially characterized. FC1 catalyzes the last step of the heme biosynthesis, i.e. the insertion of ferrous iron into protoporphyrin IX to form protoheme. The molecular function could be confirmed by functional complementation of a yeast mutant lacking ferrochelatase activity |CITS: [8175771]|. FC1 has been demonstrated to be located in chloroplasts |CITS: [11602264]| more specifically in the thylakoid and the envelope |CITS: [9753778]|. Ferrochelatase 1 is expressed in all tissues of the plant, especially high in roots and flowers |CITS: [12374307]|. The difference in expression pattern and localization between the two ferrochelatases has been interpreted as a division of the corresponding function. FC1 has been shown to be co-expressed with the entry enzyme of the |FRAME:PWY-5188|, i.e. glutamyl-tRNA reductase (HEMA2) and is involved under normal conditions in heme biosynthesis in non-photosynthetic tissues. However, under oxidative stress, FC1 together with HEMA2 is induced in photosynthetic tissues and contributes to heme supply for defensive hemoproteins such as cytochrome P450&apos;s |CITS: [17416636]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT5G26030-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT5G26030.1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Uniprot" value="P42043" />
