    <DataNode elementId="AT2G26540-MONOMER" textLabel="uroporphyrinogen III synthase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="O48721" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="450" centerY="475" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="THE-ARABIDOPSIS-INFORMATION-RESOURCE, dreher">UROS (uroporphyrinogen III synthase) catalyzes the formation of uroporphyrinogen III, an important tetrapyrrole compound that feeds into chlorophyll, heme, and siroheme biosynthesis |CITS: [18042043]|. Although this enzymatic activity is present in many species, the primary amino acid sequence for this enzyme differs significantly across kingdoms. For example, comparable enzymes in humans and &lt;i&gt;E.coli&lt;/i&gt; have only 20% amino acid identity. Therefore, the Arabidopsis version of this protein was cloned through functional complementation. Its specific enzymatic activity was then confirmed through &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; assays of purified recombinant protein |CITS: [18042043]|. It possesses the 7 invariant residues identified in uroporphyrinogen III synthases from other kingdoms, as well as 12 out of 15 of the conserved residues. It is also predicted to have structural similarity to the human protein |CITS: [18042043]|. Based on &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; assays, a recombinant form of the protein lacking the first 81 amino acids (UROS&amp;Delta;81) had an apparent specific activity of 76 +/ 3.8 &lt;i&gt;&amp;mu;&lt;/i&gt;mol h&lt;sup&gt;-1&lt;/sup&gt; mg&lt;sup&gt;-1&lt;/sup&gt; but this may have been limited by the availability of the |FRAME: HYDROXYMETHYLBILANE hydroxymethylbilane| substrate in the assay |CITS: [18042043]|. UROS has an N-terminal extension not found in other non-plant species that likely functions as an N-terminal transit peptide directing UROS entry into the chloroplast. Both &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; import assays using pea chloroplasts and &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt; UROS:GFP localization experiments using tobacco cells indicate that UROS is targetted to this plastid |CITS: [18042043]|. Though gel permeation assays using a truncated form of the protein (UROS&amp;Delta;81) detected a molecular mass of ~62 kD for this protein, indicative of potential dimerization, all of the other uroporphyrinogen III synthases studied to date function as monomers suggesting that the Arabidopsis UROS enzyme is also likely to function as a monomer |CITS: [18042043]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT2G26540-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT2G26540.1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Uniprot" value="O48721" />
      <Property key="Synonym_1" value="uroporphyrinogen-III synthase" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-17257" />
