    <DataNode elementId="AT1G09940-MONOMER" textLabel="Glutamyl-tRNA reductase 2, chloroplastic" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="P49294" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="600" centerY="555" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">Glutamyl-tRNA reductase (GluTR) is an unusual enzyme which catalyzes the NADPH-dependent reduction of the loaded tRNA Glu-tRNA&lt;sup&gt;Glu&lt;/sup&gt; to glutamate 1-semialdehyde (GSA) with the release of free tRNA&lt;sup&gt;Glu&lt;/sup&gt;. The enzyme recognizes the tRNA cofactor in a nucleotide sequence-specific fashion and does not discriminate on the basis of the activated amino acid |CITS:[7908550]|. The &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana HEMA2&lt;/i&gt; gene, identified through sequence similarity with the &lt;i&gt;HEMA1&lt;/i&gt;-encoded GluTR, was heterologously expressed in the &lt;i&gt;hemA E. coli&lt;/i&gt; strain lacking the endogenous GluTR enzyme. The &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/i&gt; gene was shown to complement the mutation, thereby confirming that &lt;i&gt;AtHEMA2&lt;/i&gt; encodes for GluTR |CITS:[8605295]|.</Comment>
